{
  // Cloudflare Workers build for the Next.js app, via @opennextjs/cloudflare.
  //
  // A spike, and named like one. Its whole job is to answer one question the docs
  // cannot: does this app actually get cache hits in a real colo? The deploy target is
  // a workers.dev subdomain, nothing in DNS moves, and Vercel stays production
  // throughout.
  //
  // Deliberately minimal, and each absence is a decision rather than a gap:
  //
  //   - **No R2 bucket, and an empty open-next.config.ts.** The adapter's incremental
  //     cache exists to serve ISR and SSG revalidation. This app has no ISR and cannot
  //     have any — a route-level `revalidate` export makes Next prerender the route
  //     at build time, which the Vercel prebuilt builder then cannot reconcile
  //     ("Unable to find lambda for route"), so every API route here is
  //     `force-dynamic` by necessity. Nothing revalidates, so there is nothing for an
  //     incremental cache to hold. The companion file exists only because 1.20.5
  //     refuses to build without it; see the note in it.
  //   - **No D1 and no queue.** Both are for `revalidateTag`/`revalidatePath`. Not
  //     called anywhere in this app.
  //   - **No `images` binding.** Nothing imports `next/image`; the chain marks and
  //     plate renders are plain `<img>` on purpose (see components/ChainIcon.tsx).
  //
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "underwater-spike",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",

  // Chosen to match the date Cloudflare's Workers Caching documentation is written
  // against, because that is the feature this config exists to switch on.
  //
  // Not today's date: a compatibility date is a promise about behaviour somebody has
  // tested, so it moves when somebody checks, not automatically.
  //
  // One knock-on worth recording, because it changes a failure mode rather than fixing
  // it: any date at or after 2025-09-01 auto-enables `node:fs`. That does *not* make
  // lib/og.tsx work — Workers' `node:fs` is an ephemeral per-request virtual
  // filesystem, not a view of deployed files — it only changes `brandFonts()` from
  // failing to resolve the module to failing to find the file. The five vendored
  // `.woff` faces still need to come from the `ASSETS` binding or from the bundle.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-21",
  "compatibility_flags": [
    // The routes declare `runtime = "nodejs"`. Required at 2024-09-23 or later.
    "nodejs_compat",
    // Lets the Worker fetch absolute URLs, which every RPC read here is.
    "global_fetch_strictly_public"
  ],

  // This block is the entire port. It is worth being explicit about why, because the
  // shape of this branch changed once when the answer turned out to be a config field.
  //
  // The problem it solves: Workers run *before* the cache, so historically a Worker's
  // own response was never stored on the strength of its `Cache-Control` — that header
  // was treated as browser-facing only. Since all 8 handlers in app/api/ are
  // `force-dynamic` and lean entirely on `cacheHeaders(sMaxAge, swr)`, and since
  // @opennextjs/cloudflare's own caching covers SSG/ISR only ("SSR route will work out
  // of the box without any caching config", i.e. uncached), every request would have
  // reached the RPC. Against an endpoint measured dropping 19 of 40 batched calls that
  // is the failure mode, not a mild regression.
  //
  // Workers Caching is read-through and sits in front of the entrypoint, and it decides
  // cacheability from the response's own `Cache-Control`. `cacheHeaders` already emits
  // `public, s-maxage=N, stale-while-revalidate=M`, and both directives are honoured —
  // so no application code changes, and Vercel and Cloudflare read the same header for
  // the same reason. That is why there is no hand-rolled Cache API layer here: the
  // lower-level `caches.default` cannot read through, cannot collapse concurrent
  // requests, and cannot participate in tiered caching. Cloudflare's own guidance is to
  // prefer this for new Workers.
  //
  // Two consequences to hold onto:
  //
  //   - **`cross_version_cache` is on deliberately.** By default the Worker version is
  //     part of the cache key, so every deploy starts from an empty cache. That is the
  //     single most common reason a hit rate looks terrible, and it would make the
  //     measurement this spike exists for read ~0% on the first pass.
  //   - **Enabling this bills requests that are otherwise free**, static asset requests
  //     among them. Cache hits are charged at the standard request rate but consume no
  //     CPU time, which is the trade that matters for handlers that spend their life
  //     waiting on an RPC. Worth re-checking against the 182 asset files this build
  //     emits if request volume ever dwarfs API traffic.
  //
  // Measurement is `Cf-Cache-Status`: HIT, MISS, BYPASS (uncacheable — `no-store` or
  // `private`), EXPIRED, REVALIDATED, or UPDATING while serving inside the
  // stale-while-revalidate window. Requires wrangler >= 4.69.0; `cross_version_cache`
  // requires >= 4.107.0. Both are satisfied by the pinned 4.127.1.
  "cache": {
    "enabled": true,
    "cross_version_cache": true
  },

  // **No `limits` block, and not by choice.** Recorded because it is the one hard
  // platform wall this spike hit, and because the obvious fix is unavailable here.
  //
  // The share cards are the only thing in this app that computes rather than waits:
  // a render is Satori laying out with Yoga plus resvg encoding a 1200×630 PNG.
  // Measured on the default limit via `wrangler tail --format json`:
  // `"outcome": "exceededCpu"`, `"cpuTime": 2010`, three × "Worker exceeded CPU time
  // limit." The client sees 503, or 524 when the kill lands mid-stream — and per
  // Cloudflare neither is ever cached, so every crawler re-pays for the same failure.
  //
  // `"limits": { "cpu_ms": 30000 }` is the documented remedy and this account cannot
  // use it: the API rejects the deploy outright with *"CPU limits are not supported
  // for the Free plan"* (code 100328). Not a warning and not a silent downgrade — the
  // whole `wrangler deploy` fails, so leaving the block in breaks every deploy.
  //
  // Two of the three cards no longer need it regardless, because they were never
  // supposed to render at runtime: `/opengraph-image` and `/waterdrop/opengraph-image`
  // are prerendered at build time, and now actually get *served* that way — see
  // open-next.config.ts, which is the real fix. What remains is
  // `/token/[address]/opengraph-image`, which genuinely cannot be prerendered since
  // token addresses are unbounded. On Free that card is over the CPU ceiling and
  // there is no config that changes it; it needs the Paid plan this spike was
  // costing out anyway, or a cheaper card.
  //
  // Worth being explicit that this is CPU, not wall time. Every other handler here is
  // an RPC read that idles, which is the whole reason Workers' meter suits this app,
  // and idling needs no raise.

  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },

  "observability": {
    // On for the spike specifically: a hit-rate measurement is worth nothing if a
    // miss and an error look the same from outside.
    "enabled": true
  }
}
